  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protecting the rights of children in the Shea Supply Chain in West Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The first phase of the project entailed a research to identify potential risks and causes of child labour in the shea sector. The research focused on the shea chain in West Africa: the harvesting, washing, drying, grinding, processing and transportation of the nuts. Contrary to big commercial coffee and cocoa plantations, shea nuts only grow naturally. And the harvesting is done almost exclusively by women. This research was carried out in in Mali, Burkina Faso and Ghana, and determined follow-up recommendations and proposed actions for the second phase in which five identified possible root causes, like existing cultural norms, of child labour will be addressed.Four large international companies active in the shea industry, Bunge, Fuji Europe Africa, Wilmar/Olenex and AAK together with West African non-governmental organisations (NGOs), PAS (Ghana), a to be selected Burkinabe NGO and Cordaid form the consortium.The consortium will jointly develop and implement a strategy to address the causes and reduce the risk of child labour in two pilot areas in Ghana and in Burkina as well. Tackling child labour can only be done effectively if all stakeholders in an area (e.g. large international companies, governments and civil society organizations) work together in close collaboration with the communities were shea is harvested, and share learnings from the pilot activities. This strategy of cooperation will be followed in all the pilots in the two countries.This project supports companies to comply with recent Dutch legislation. The Child Labour Due Diligence Act imposes a duty of care on companies that supply goods and services to consumers in the Netherlands to prevent child labour within their production chains. European legislation is also imposing companies to prevent human rights violations and environmental risks and impacts within their own activities and supply chains.</narrative>
  </description>
